    "sha256": "0a7ad7e7916d20d22fd2e5ac30c0188a08a5aaf2c598cb66c4b04cb35ee9459e",
    "text": "All evidence is gathered and verified. Here is the analysis.\n\n# Setup: what `zvec.init(optimize_threads=4)` establishes\n\n- `python/zvec/zvec.py:162-163,173` \u2014 `optimize_threads` goes into a config dict passed to `Initialize`.\n- `src/binding/python/model/common/python_config.cc:154-157` \u2014 it becomes `ConfigData::optimize_thread_count = 4` (must be positive); `python_config.cc:193` \u2014 `GlobalConfig::Instance().initialize(data)`.\n- `src/db/common/global_resource.cc:21-29` \u2014 a `std::call_once` initializer constructs the **global optimize pool**: `new ailego::ThreadPool(GlobalConfig::Instance().optimize_thread_count(), ...)`; accessor at `src/db/common/global_resource.h:31-34`.\n- `src/ailego/parallel/thread_pool.cc:85-101` \u2014 the `ThreadPool(size, binding)` constructor clamps `size` to `[1, hardware_concurrency()]` and eagerly spawns that many worker threads. With `hardware_concurrency() \u2265 4`, the global optimize pool has exactly **4 workers**. (Default without `init` would be the cgroup CPU limit, `src/db/common/config.cc:37,42`.)\n\n`OptimizeOption(concurrency=N)` maps to `OptimizeOptions::concurrency_` (`src/include/zvec/db/options.h:60-62`, default 0; pybind at `src/binding/python/model/param/python_param.cc:2178-2202`), and `CollectionImpl::optimize` threads it into every compact/create-index task (`src/db/collection.cc:952,989,1160-1207,761-772`), which reach the segment merge code.\n\n# 1. Pool selection per invocation\n\nBoth db-layer merge entry points apply identical logic \u2014 `SegmentImpl::merge_vector_indexer` (`src/db/index/segment/segment.cc:1560-1567`) and `SegmentHelper::MergeWithOptionalReuse` (`src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:804-811`, called at `segment_helper.cc:668,695,743`):\n\n- **A (`concurrency=0`)**: `merge_options.pool = GlobalResource::Instance().optimize_thread_pool()` (the shared global pool) and `write_concurrency = pool->count()` = 4. In `Index::merge` (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1436-1439`), because `options.pool != nullptr`, the reducer gets `set_thread_pool(options.pool)` \u2014 **the 4-worker global pool itself**, with `effective_write_concurrency = min(4, 4) = 4`.\n- **B (`concurrency=2`)**: `merge_options.pool` stays `nullptr`, `write_concurrency = 2`. In `Index::merge` (`index.cc:1440-1446`), a **call-local** `ailego::ThreadPool(2, false)` is created (2 workers), given to the reducer, and destroyed when `merge` returns (declared at `index.cc:1433-1434` with an explicit lifetime comment).\n\n**B does not change the global pool.** The global pool is built once under `std::call_once` (`global_resource.cc:22-23`); B never touches it \u2014 it borrows nothing from it and leaves its 4 workers idle. The two invocations use disjoint pools: shared-global-4 (A) vs ephemeral-local-2 (B).\n\n# 2. Handoff to the target builder\n\n`MixedStreamerReducer::reduce` \u2192 `reduce_with_builder` (`src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:206-230,672-739`) \u2192 `index_build` (`mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:742-777`):\n\n- Line 743-744: the reducer's pool is wrapped as `BorrowedSingleQueueIndexThreads(*thread_pool_)` \u2014 an adapter that **borrows** the existing `ailego::ThreadPool` without spawning threads (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_threads.h:174-225`; `count()` returns the underlying pool's worker count).\n- Lines 745-747: if a `target_builder_converter_` exists, the **conversion-training call** `IndexConverter::TrainAndTransform(converter, target_holder, threads)` receives that same wrapper; the implementation forwards it: `converter->train(two_pass_holder, std::move(threads))` (`src/core/framework/index_converter.cc:27-36`; overload declared at `src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_converter.h:236-239`).\n- Line 760: `target_builder_->train(threads, target_holder)`.\n- Line 768: `target_builder_->build(std::move(threads), target_holder)`.\n\nSo for A, all three calls receive a borrowed view of the 4-worker global pool; for B, a borrowed view of the 2-worker local pool. **Passing vs. consuming**: this proves the pool is *offered* to the converter-train, train, and build entry points as an `IndexThreads::Pointer`; whether a given implementation submits work to it is per-implementation. DiskAnnBuilder does consume it \u2014 e.g. `build_internal`/`prune_internal` submit exactly `threads->count()` tasks (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:304-315,341-352`) and `generate_quantized_data` stripes work by `threads->count()` (`diskann_builder.cc:451-479`). A counterexample showing \"passed \u2260 used\" is the trainer-based no-op overload `DiskAnnBuilder::train(const IndexTrainer::Pointer&)` (`diskann_builder.cc:616-631`), which ignores threading entirely.\n\n# 3. DiskANN PQ training: a fresh pool, sized by min()\n\nThe PQ chunk-dispatch pool is **not** a reuse of the caller's pool:\n\n- `DiskAnnBuilder::train(threads, holder)` (`diskann_builder.cc:633-681`): if `threads` were null it would create its own `SingleQueueIndexThreads(build_thread_count_, false)` (657-663); here it uses the passed wrapper, then calls `train_quantized_data(threads)` (665).\n- `train_quantized_data` (`diskann_builder.cc:378-427`): line 391 carries the explicit comment *\"The quantizer creates its own pool, so bound it by the caller's pool.\"* It computes `pq_thread_count = max(1, min(build_thread_count_, threads->count()))` (392-393) and passes it as the `\"thread_count\"` **init param** (394-396). The subsequent `quantizer_->train(training_holder)` (406) takes **no threads argument** \u2014 only the derived count travels, not the pool object.\n- `build_thread_count_` is the builder's own setting: read from `PARAM_DISKANN_BUILDER_THREAD_COUNT` = `\"zvec.diskann.builder.thread_count\"` (`diskann_builder.cc:51`; `src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_params.h:31-32`); if 0, it defaults to `hardware_concurrency()` (`diskann_builder.cc:53-55`).\n- `PqInt8Quantizer::init` stores the param into `thread_count_` (`src/turbo/quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer.cc:142`; default 0 at `pq_int8_quantizer.h:217`). Then `train_all_chunks` \u2014 reached from `train` at `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:298,305` \u2014 constructs a **brand-new** `SingleQueueIndexThreads(thread_count_, false)` (322-323) and dispatches one strided task per pool worker across the PQ chunks (330-343), waiting at 356.\n\nSo in both scenarios the PQ outer pool is a temporary pool of its own: A gets `min(build_thread_count_, 4)` workers, B gets `min(build_thread_count_, 2)` (each \u2265 1). With the default `build_thread_count_ = hardware_concurrency() \u2265 4`, that is 4 for A and 2 for B. The caller's borrowed pool is used only as a *size bound*, never as the execution vehicle; its workers are idle during PQ training.\n\n# 4. What these settings do and do not establish\n\n**They establish (worker-pool capacity):**\n- A: the merge's builder-stage work runs on the shared global optimize pool with exactly 4 workers (`init(optimize_threads=4)` \u2192 `global_resource.cc:27-29` + clamp `thread_pool.cc:93-101`); the PQ training pool is capped at 4 via the `min()` at `diskann_builder.cc:392-393`.\n- B: the merge runs on a call-local pool with exactly 2 workers (`index.cc:1441-1445`); the PQ pool is capped at 2; the global 4-worker pool exists but is untouched and unused.\n- In both cases, task fan-out through these pools is bounded by the pool's `count()` (e.g. `diskann_builder.cc:312-315`, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:332-341`).\n\n**They do not establish:**\n- **Total process thread count.** These settings bound only the pools they configure. Other threads coexist regardless: the global query pool (`global_resource.cc:24-26`), the per-call local pool in B (transient, additive to the global 4), freshly spawned `SingleQueueIndexThreads` pools inside PQ training (and any other quantizer that self-pools, e.g. `src/turbo/quantizer/pq_fast_quantizer/pq_fast_quantizer.cc:347-348`), plus Python interpreter, logging, and OS/runtime threads. Nothing here caps the sum.\n- **Actual CPU utilization.** Pool size is a concurrency ceiling, not a usage guarantee: workers idle when queues are empty; strided dispatch may under-fill the pool (e.g. PQ submits `pool_count` tasks but if `num_chunk_ < pool_count` some tasks do no work, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:332-341`); stages that ignore the passed pool (like the quantizer's own-pool path, or the no-op `train(IndexTrainer)` overload) shift work elsewhere; and thread creation, cgroup limits, and binding behavior (`thread_pool.cc:32-59,93-101`) affect how configured workers map to cores. Proving utilization would require measurement, which is outside these settings' semantics."
  },
